  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The emissions forecasts rely on a 'no-action' baseline and trade demand projections, which may not capture sudden shifts in international maritime regulations or global trade patterns.",
      "what": "A bottom-up model of 35,000 merchant vessels was developed to forecast fleet composition and CO2 emissions up to the year 2050, using technical, operational, and historical trade demand data. The model shows that under a 'no-action' scenario, even low-growth traffic will fail to meet carbon neutrality goals, requiring fleet renewal rates of up to 2,000 vessels annually starting in 2040. Alternatively, retrofitting existing ships could address up to 40% of total shipping tonnage to bridge the gap.",
      "why": "It quantifies the specific scale of shipyard capacity and fleet renewal rates required for maritime decarbonization, demonstrating that retrofitting is a structural necessity rather than an optional measure if early planning targets are missed."
    }
  },
